## 1. Introduction
Trivalent lanthanide ions have special photoactive properties such as sharp emission spectra for high color purity, broad emission bands covering ultraviolet–visible–near infrared region, a wide range of lifetimes from microsecond to second, and high luminescence quantum efficiencies. These advantages make them to have practical applications such as lighting devices and biomedical analysis [1–3]. However, the f–f transitions of lanthanide ions are spin forbidden so that it is difficult to generate efficient luminescence emission by direct excitation. In order to solve this trouble, the common method is to dope lanthanide ions into special matrices [4,5] or coordinate to organic ligands [6,7].
For the latter, luminescent lanthanide complexes possess the poor stability, which limits their practical application [6,7].
Inorganic–organic hybrid materials combine inorganic and organic components at a molecular or nanometer scale, which provides certain strong points of organic compounds with the advantages of inorganic components [8–11]. Moreover, the composition of hybrids may realize the functional integration to produce new property for application, which is particularly benefit for photofunctional fields [12]. To presence, the chemically bonded hybrids have attracted great interest for their advantage for strong interactions such as covalent, ion–covalent, coordination and Lewis acid/ base bonds [13–16]. And the focus has shifted to the introduction of crystalline building blocks into hybrid systems to combine inorganic–organic hybrids and nanocomposites, which can show the luminescence behaviors of both inorganic phosphors and molecular materials [17–20]. So far, two important applications have been developed: one is as sensors or labels in biomedicine [21], which depends on controlling the scale and structure of the hybrids; the other is luminescent devices, depending on the preparation technology and functional integration [22].
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are an important class of hybrid inorganic–organic materials [23,24], which have emerged as promising hybrids in many areas of gas sorption, catalysis, separation, drug delivery, luminescence sensor and biological-imaging [25–27]. MOFs belong to inorganic–organic hybrid solids with porous crystalline structures and can be synthesized with a wide range of metal ions and organic ligands [28]. The versatile choice of metal ions and organic ligands as building blocks provides the great potential to embody unique performance for their host–guest interactions within the framework [29]. Besides, MOFs display a wide range of luminescent performance resulting from the multifaceted nature of their structure [30]. The hybrid nature of MOF materials involves both an organic ligand and a metal ion within the special porous structure [31,32]. This enables a wide range of emissive phenomena to be found, which is classified by Allendorf and Bauerin according to the luminescence origin: linker-based luminescence, the coordinated metal ions based luminescence, antenna effects for energy transfer and sensitization luminescence, excimer and exciplex formation emission, and so on [33].
It is worthy pointing out that luminescent lanthanide ions are emphasized in MOF based hybrid materials. Lanthanide ions themselves behave as the central metal ions to form all kinds of luminescent MOFs [34,35]. On the other hand, lanthanide ions also can be fabricated to MOFs as an active species to form luminescent lanthanide functionalized MOFs [36–39]. In both of the two kinds of MOFs based hybrid materials, the introduction or existence of lanthanide ions make the whole hybrid system possess complicated, possibly involving inker-based, metal-based luminescence, antenna effects luminescence or guest molecule luminescence [34–39]. Besides, lanthanide species can compensate with MOFs or ligands as the luminescent species for their different luminescent region, which is favorable for tuning multi-color and integrating white color emission [40–43].
Bio-MOFs, metal-adeninate organic frameworks reported by Rosi’s group are appealing candidates for practical applications because of their permanent microporosity, high surface areas and chemical stability due to the presence of basic bio-molecule building units [44]. In particular, $\mathrm{Zn_{8}(a d)_{4}(B P D C)_{6}O\cdot2M e_{2}N H_{2}}$ (where ad $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ adeninate, BPDC $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ biphenyldicarboxylate, denoted as bio-MOF-1), which are interconnected via biphenyl-dicarboxylate linkers [45], which can be further functionalized via post-synthetic cation exchange of luminescent lanthanide ions [46].
In this paper, considering the possibility of the functionalization of bio-MOF-1 exchanged by lanthanide ions, we want to prepare the lanthanide ion modified bio-MOF-1 by ionic exchange and further introduce some ligands (TTA) by gas diffusion (‘‘ship-in bottle’’) to improve the luminescence firstly. Then we try to tune the luminescence color and even integrate the close white luminescence. Moreover, we study the application of lanthanide functionalized bio-MOF-1 in the sensing of metal ions
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Staring materials
Bio-MOF-1 was synthesized according to the reported procedure [45]. Solutions of $\mathrm{Tb}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and $\mathrm{Eu}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ in acetonitrile were obtained by dissolving their oxides $\mathrm{Tb}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{7}$ and $\mathtt{E u}_{2}0_{3}$ in nitric acid, followed by evaporation and solvation in acetonitrile solvents. 2-Thenoyltrifluroacetone $99\%$ , TTA, Adamas), N, $\mathsf{N}^{\prime}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.9\%$ , Aladdin) were used as received. All the other reagents are analytical pure and purchased from China National Medicines Group.
## 2.2. Preparation of $L n^{3+}@$ bio-MOF-1 and TTA-Eu3+@bio-MOF-1
The loading of lanthanide ions onto bio-MOF-1 was performed by cation exchange [46]. The as-synthesized bio-MOF-1 was soaked in the acetonitrile solutions of $\mathsf{L n}^{3+}$ nitrate: ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ ( $\mathrm{1.8\mmol{L}^{-1}},$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ $\cdot5.0\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ), respectively at $65^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Then the resulting products were washed with ethanol three times and dried at $353\mathrm{K}$ under normal atmospheric conditions. In order to introduce TTA into $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ , we used an encapsulation method called ‘‘ship-in-bottle’’ by TTA gas diffusion, similar to modified zeolite. $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ was kept in the vapor of TTA at $313\mathrm{K}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , whose choice of temperature depends on the sublimation of TTA. The resulting product was washed with acetonitrile, centrifuged twice and finally dried at $353\mathrm{K}$ under normal atmospheric conditions. The elemental analyses data were shown in supporting information.
Fig. 1 shows the selected scheme for the synthesis procedure of Eu3+@bio-MOF-1 and the encapsulation of TTA with ‘‘ship-inbottle’’ method. Through the cation exchange to functionalize bio-MOF-1, three kinds of visible light emissive lanthanide ions can be incorporated into bio-MOF-1 host [46]. Here the formation of $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ in bio-MOF system provide the possibility to realize the cation exchange reaction. After that, TTA ligand can be further introduced with a ‘‘ship-in-bottle’ method used in the functionalization of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ [47]. The vapor diffusion of TTA ligand will coordinates to lanthanide ions in the pore of bioMOF-1. Similarly to the method to synthesize ${\tt E u}^{3+}$ -bio-MOF-1 hybrids, some metal ions can be introduced into solution of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ -bio-MOF-1, whose sensing property can be checked from the luminescent spectra of the mixed solution system.
## 2.3. Physical characterization
Fourier transform infrared spectra (FTIR) were measured with KBr slices from 4000 to $400\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ using a Nexus 912AO446 infrared spectrum radiometer. The contents of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions $\mathrm{\DeltaLn=Eu}$ , Tb) and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in the hybrids were determined with ICP-AES. The elemental analyses of nitrogen element the hybrids are measured with a CARIO-ERBA 1106 elemental analyzer. X-ray powder diffraction patterns (XRD) were acquired on a D8 Foucs (Bruker) with $\mathtt{C u}\mathtt{K}\mathtt{Q}$ radiation Field-emission; the data were collected within $2\theta$ range of $3\substack{-45^{\circ}}$ . Scanning electronic microscope (SEM) images were obtained with a Hitachi S-4800. Nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms were measured at the liquid nitrogen temperature, using a Nova 1000 analyzer. Surface areas were calculated by the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) method. UV diffused reflectance spectra were received with BWS003 (B&W Tek).
## 2.4. Luminescent measurements and sensing study
The luminescence spectra were recorded on an Edinburgh FL920 phosphorimeter using a 450 W xenon lamp as excitation source. We detect bio-MOF-1 sensing metal ions aqueous solution. The outer luminescent quantum efficiency was determined using an integrating sphere ( $150\mathrm{mm}$ diameter, $\mathsf{B a S O}_{4}$ coating) from Edinburgh FLS920 phosphorimeter. The spectra were corrected for variations in the output of the excitation source and for variations in the detector response. The quantum yield can be defined as the integrated intensity of the luminescence signal divided by the integrated intensity of the absorption signal. The absorption intensity was calculated by subtracting the integrated intensity of the light source with the sample in the integrating sphere from the integrated intensity of the light source with a blank sample in the integrating sphere. In addition, we test $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ sensing metal ions DMF solution because of its stronger signal than the other two MOFs.
The bio-MOF-1 was immersed in various metal ionnitrates $\mathbf{\widetilde{M}^{n+}}=\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ , $\mathsf{M g}^{+}$ , $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{d}}^{2+}$ , $C0^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C r}}^{3+}$ , $A|^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ) aqueous solution for luminescence studies, whose concentrations were all controlled to $2.0\times10^{-3}\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . The same concentration was controlled when detecting the sensing of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ on DMF solution metal ionnitrates.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Physical characterization
The selected X-ray diffraction patterns for parent bio-MOF-1 and $\ln^{3+}\ @$ bio-MOF-1 $\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Eu}$ , Tb) hybrids are shown in Fig. 2. When loading $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ into bio-MOF-1, the XRD diffraction peaks are still retained, suggesting the incorporation of lanthanide ions have no apparent influence on the framework structure of bio-MOF-1 and the crystallinity of bio-MOF-1 is good. Certainly the pore structure of the functionalized bio-MOF is also maintained. The crystal framework of bio-MOF-1 is stable and does not be destroyed, which is favorable for the further application.
Fig. S1 shows the selected SEM image of bio-MOF-1 and $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@$ bio-MOF-1 hybrids, which presents the similar irregular schistose morphology. Fig. S2 shows the selected FT-IR spectra of bio-MOF-1 and $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}@\mathrm{bio-MOF-1}.$ . Both of them show the similar absorption bands. The absorption bands at 1540 and $1380\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are stretching vibration of benzene and carboxylate anion, respectively. The inset of the zoom bands at range of $1350{-}1700\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ further clearly show the IR spectra of bio-MOF-1 and $\ln^{3+}(\varpi$ bio-MOF-1 are almost identical. This means that the loading $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ into the bio-MOF-1 framework have not change the chemical linking modes of bio-MOF-1, which takes agreement with the result of XRD in Fig. 2.
The selected TGA plot of bio-MOF-1 (as shown in Fig. S3) reveals a weight loss from $80^{\circ}C$ to $115^{\circ}\mathsf C$ which can be attributed to the loss of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ in bio-MOF-1. Thereafter, there is a weight loss of $11.5\%$ from 160 to $260^{\circ}\mathsf C$ because of the decomposition of DMF and dimethylammonium cations in the channels. Additionally, when it comes to Eu@bio-MOF-1, we just observe $8\%$ weight loss of water from 80 to $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$ Corresponding to the cation exchange from dimethylammonium cations to lanthanide ions, we cannot find the decomposition of DMF or dimethylammonium cations.
Fig. S3 shows the ultraviolet–visible diffuse reflectance spectra of three hybrids $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}@\mathrm{bio-MOF-1}$ $\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Eu}$ , Tb, $\mathrm{Eu/Tb)}$ , which show the similar wide spectra bands at the range of $200{-}350\mathrm{nm}$ . This is identical to bio-MOF-1, suggesting that the energy donor is bioMOF-1 itself in the hybrid system.
## 3.2. Luminescence properties
Fig. S4 presents the luminescent excitation and emission spectra of bio-MOF-1. The excitation spectrum shows a similar broad excitation band ranged in $250\mathrm{-}400\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the absorption of organic ligand BPDC in the framework of bioMOF-1. Its emission spectrum presents a broad band covering $350\mathrm{-}550\mathrm{nm}$ with maximum peak at around $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ . This is a high energy level near ultraviolet emission with deep blue color. Fig. 3 presents the luminescent excitation and emission spectra of hybrid systems $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ (a) and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}@$ bio-MOF-1 (b), both of them show a similar broad excitation band ranged in $250-$ $400\mathrm{nm}$ originating from the absorption of BPDC in the framework of bio-MOF-1. So this suggests that the energy derives from the absorption of bio-MOF-1. After the incorporation of ${\tt E u}^{3+}$ , its characteristic luminescent spectrum bands appear, and the luminescence band for bio-MOF-1 still exists. For $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ hybrids in Fig. 3(b), five strong emission bands at around 579, 590, 614, 647 and $695\mathrm{nm}$ can be found for the characteristic ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{J}\left(J=0{-}5\right)$ transition of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ . For $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}@\mathrm{bio-MOF-}1$ hybrids in Fig. 3(b), four weak emission bands at around 490, 544, 583 and $622\mathrm{nm}$ appear for the characteristic $^5\mathrm{D}_{4}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{J}\left(J=6,5,4,3\right)$ transition of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ . $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}@\mathrm{bio-MOF-}1$ hybrids still show the strong emission of bio-MOF-1, resulting in the deep blue color luminescence, like bio-MOF-1 itself. While $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@$ bio-MOF-1 hybrid system only shows very weak emission of bio-MOF-1, suggesting that the effective energy transfer take place between bio-MOF-1 and ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ . This will be proved by the following energy transfer efficiency.
Fig. S5 inspects the introduction of TTA ligand on the luminescence of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ functionalized bio-MOF-1. It is found that the introduction of TTA ligand for $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{bio-MOF}-1$ hybrid system can improve the luminescent intensity of ${\tt E u}^{3+}$ , while the emission intensity of bio-MOF is hardly changed. This fact reveals that the increase of emission of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ is originated from the sensitization of coordinated TTA ligand. By ‘‘ship-in-bottle’’ method, TTA can be dispersed into bio-MOF-1 to link with ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ through coordination bonds. So MOF can also be loaded with lanthanide ions or ligands like microporous zeolites.
Fig. 4 shows the emission spectrum of double lanthanide ions $(\mathsf{E u}^{3+}\mathsf{T b}^{3+})$ functionalized bio-MOF-1 hybrid system. Considering the ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ functionalized bio-MOF-1 system possesses a stronger emission than $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ one (see Fig. 3(a) and (b)), so the excitation spectrum is obtained by monitoring the $613\mathrm{nm}$ emission of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ and similar to that of single ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ or $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ exchanged bio-MOF-1 system. By checking its emission spectrum, it can be clearly found that both ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ can be incorporated into bio-MOF-1 host with the same exchange reaction. The characteristic emissions of the two lanthanide ions are shown in Fig. 4. The two peaks at 490 and $544\mathrm{nm}$ are for the ${^5\mathrm{D}}_{4}\to{^7\mathrm{F}}_{6}$ and ${^5\mathrm{D}}_{4}\rightarrow{^7\mathrm{F}}_{5}$ transition of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ . Another two peaks at 650 and $700\mathrm{nm}$ are for $^5\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow^{7}\mathrm{F}_{3}$ and ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{4}$ transition of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ . The remaining three emission peaks at around 580, 590 and $615\mathrm{nm}$ are mainly from ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{J}\:(J=0,$ 1, 2) transition of ${\tt E u}^{3+}$ , overlapped with the ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{4}\rightarrow{}^{7}\dot{\mathrm{F}}_{6}$ and $^5\mathrm{D}_{4}\rightarrow^{7}\mathrm{F}_{5}$ transitions of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ . This can be clearly seen from the widening of the emission band at maximum of $614\mathrm{nm}$ . In addition, the characteristic emission of bio-MOF-1 still can be observed, similar to the single lanthanide functionalized bio-MOF-1 hybrids.
Fig. 5 shows the CIE diagrams of bio-MOF1 and its hybrid system $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}@$ bio-MOF-1. Calculating the emission spectra data of Figs. 3 and 4 and Figs. S4 and S5, the CIE coordinate can be determined and draw the CIE diagrams (see Fig. S5). Among bio-MOF-1 and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}@\mathrm{bio-MOF-}1$ hybrid system are located in deep blue region from bio-MOF-1. While $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ hybrid system is in the purple-reddish region for the luminescent integration of both ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ and bio-MOF-1. For $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}@^{\cdot}$ bio-MOF-1 hybrid system, its CIE coordinate is close to white region (showing light pink color). The detailed data for CIE coordinates are summarized in Table 1.
Moreover, the absolute luminescent quantum yield data are obtained with an integrating sphere and a calibrated detector setup for solid materials. The resulting absolute luminescence quantum yields are listed in Table 1. For parent bio-MOF-1, its quantum efficiency is $17.9\%$ $(\eta_{\mathrm{D}})$ . After introduction of lanthanide ions, the quantum efficiencies of bio-MOF-1 emission in $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}@\mathrm{bio-MOF-1}$ hybrid system decrease, which is due to the energy transfer from bio-MOF-1 and $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ (as shown in emission spectra). In the energy transfer system, bio-MOF-1 behaves as energy donor and $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ act as energy accepter. Therefore, the lower the quantum efficiency of hybrid system, the higher the energy transfer efficiency. On the basis of the Refs. [48,49], the energy transfer efficiency $\eta_{\mathrm{ET}}$ from bio-MOF-1 to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ can be determined with the following equation:
$$
\eta_{\mathrm{ET}}=1-\eta/\eta_{\mathrm{D}}
$$
Here $\eta_{\mathrm{D}}$ and $\eta$ are the luminescent quantum yields of energy donor (bio-MOF-1) and energy donor–acceptor $(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}@\mathrm{bio-MOF-}$ 1), respectively. Subsequently, the energy transfer efficiency $\eta_{\mathrm{ET}}$ from bio-MOF-1 to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@$ bio-MOF-1 $(66.5\%)$ is higher than that from bio-MOF-1 to $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\ (32.4\%)$ . This is in agreement with their emission spectra. Besides, the energy transfer efficiency of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@$ bio-MOF-1 is similar to that of TTA- $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\cdot}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Eu}}^{3\boldsymbol{\cdot}}(\varpi^{}$ bio-MOF-1, indicating the energy transfer between bio-MOF-1 and ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ are similar and so the enhancement of the emission in TTA$\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@$ bio-MOF-1 is due to TTA ligand.
## 3.3. Luminescent sensing
To examine the potential of bio-MOF-1 for sensing metal ions, the relative luminescent intensities in the aqueous solutions of different metal ions are shown in Fig. 6, whose data are collected from the corresponding spectra in Fig. S6. Bio-MOF-1 is dispersed in aqueous solution as the standard suspension under the scan slit of $4\mathrm{nm}$ . From Fig. S6 and Fig. 6, it can be seen the introduction of $\mathsf{C r}^{3+}$ , $A|^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ , $\mathbf{Mg}^{+}$ , $C o^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{d}}^{2+}$ hardly changes the luminescent intensity of bio-MOF-1. $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ have different content of apparent quenching effect on the luminescent intensity of bio-MOF-1. It is worthy pointing out that $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ shows the high selectivity, which quenches the luminescent intensity of bio-MOF-1 remarkably. From the in-set photograph, it can be clearly seen the luminescent quenching of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ on the emission of bio-MOF-1 (pink-red color almost disappear). The selectivity for sensing metal ions may be due to the exchange interaction between different metal ions and bio-MOF-1 host.
Furtherly, we check the sensing of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ hybrid system for different metal ions (in DMF solution), which we maintain the scan slit of $3\mathrm{nm}$ and use the DMF solution of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@$ bio-MOF1as a standard suspension. The relative intensities of ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2}$ at $616\mathrm{{nm}}$ from their suspension-state photoluminescent spectra are shown in Fig. 7. $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ , $\mathbf{Mg^{+}}$ and $C0^{2+}$ has slight quenching effect of luminescence of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ hybrids, while $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ shows a slight enhancement of luminescent intensity of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@$ bio-MOF-1 hybrids. Both $\mathsf{C r}^{3+}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ produce the markedly quenching of the luminescent intensity of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ hybrids. Especially the introduction of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ almost quenches the luminescence of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@$ bio-MOF-1 hybrids. These results may be related to the interaction between different metal ions and bioMOF-1 and exchange of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ . When we check the ${\mathsf{E u}}^{3+}$ content of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@$ bio-MOF-1 after sensing on $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , it decreases from $1.52\%$ to $1.29\%$ suggesting the exchange reaction between ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ . A deep study needs to be performed.
Fig. 8 displays the emission spectra (a) and $K_{s\nu}$ curve (b) of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ in the various concentrations of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ aqueous solutions under the excitation at $317\mathrm{nm}$ . For better understanding the response of fluorescence of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ion, we also examined the quenching effect with different concentration of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ranging from 0 to $5\times10^{-5}\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . In Fig. 8a, the luminescence intensity of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ decreases gradually while the concentration of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ is increasing. When the concentration reaches to $1\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , the emission intensity of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ is sharply weakened. Fig. 8b shows the $K_{s\nu}$ curve of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ with $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ . The linear correlation coefficient $(R)$ is 0.994 with the liner range from 0 to $500\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . The $K_{s\nu}$ value is calculated as $3.38\times10^{3}$ , which reveals a strong quenching effect on the $\mathtt{E u}^{3\mathrm{+}}@$ bio-MOF-1 luminescence.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, lanthanide ions are encapsulated into a special metal organic framework, bio-MOF-1 to assemble luminescent hybrid system via postsynthetic cation exchange. Multi-color can be obtained by adjusting the luminescence of both bio-MOF-1 host and lanthanide ions. The introduction of TTA by a ‘‘ship-in-bottle’’ (gas diffusion) method can enhance the luminescent intensity of ${\mathsf{E u}}^{3+}$ for their coordination interaction. Besides, ${\mathsf{E u}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ can be incorporated into same bio-MOF-1 system to integrate the close white color emission. On the other hand, both bio-MOF-1 and $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathtt{b i o-M O F-}1$ hybrids are tried to study the luminescent sensing of metal ions in aqueous or DMF solution, which presents highly sensitive sensing for $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ . All these results provide useful data for the further practical application of lanthanide functionalized MOF in the fields of optical devices or sensors.
